# Unity performance rules

- WARN: GetComponent<T>() called inside Update, FixedUpdate, or LateUpdate. Cache in Awake instead.
- WARN: FindObjectOfType or FindObjectsOfType anywhere. They are obsolete in Unity 6; use FindFirstObjectByType, FindAnyObjectByType, or FindObjectsByType.
- WARN: GameObject.Find or any Find*ObjectByType call in Update or other per-frame code. Look references up once (Awake or Start) and cache them, or use serialized references or events.
- WARN: String concatenation with + in Update. Use StringBuilder or TextMeshPro.SetText.
- WARN: new List, new Dictionary, or any heap allocation inside Update. Pre-allocate and reuse.
- WARN: LINQ queries (Where, Select, OrderBy) in per-frame code. Use manual loops.
- WARN: Instantiate/Destroy called frequently. Use object pooling instead.
- INFO: Camera.main is cached by the engine (since 2020.2), so it is cheap, but still cache it in a field when used many times per frame.
- WARN: SendMessage or BroadcastMessage. Use direct references, events, or interfaces.
- WARN: Resources.Load in runtime code. Use Addressables.LoadAssetAsync instead.
- INFO: Use the Profiler to verify actual bottlenecks before optimizing.
- INFO: Use Burst compiler and Jobs for CPU-intensive work.
- INFO: Use NonAlloc physics methods (RaycastNonAlloc, OverlapSphereNonAlloc) to avoid per-frame allocations.
